  <h1>The cheapest way to get from San Francisco to Los Angeles (2026)</h1>
  <div class="meta">Updated July 2026 · ~380 miles · about 6 hours by road</div>

  <p class="lede">San Francisco to Los Angeles is one of the most-traveled routes in the country, and you've got five real ways to make it: fly, drive solo, take the bus, take the train, or carpool. Here's what each actually costs — all-in, not just the sticker price — and which one wins for your trip.</p>

  <h2>Quick comparison</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>All-in cost</th><th>Door-to-door time</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Bus (FlixBus / Greyhound)</td><td class="win">$20–$50</td><td>8–9 hrs</td><td>Lowest ticket, if you have the time</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Carpool (Seat Sherpa)</td><td>$40–$55 / seat</td><td class="win">~6 hrs</td><td>Cheap + fast, dropped near your destination</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Drive solo</td><td>$50–$70 gas + parking</td><td>~6 hrs</td><td>Total flexibility, if you don't mind driving</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fly</td><td>$60–$150 + bags</td><td>4–5 hrs</td><td>Fastest, if you catch a cheap fare</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Train (Amtrak)</td><td>$50–$120</td><td>11+ hrs</td><td>The scenery — not the speed</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The bus: cheapest ticket, longest day</h2>
  <p>FlixBus and Greyhound run SF → LA for as little as $20–$50 if you book ahead. The catch is time: 8–9 hours on a fixed schedule, plus getting to and from the bus terminals on each end. If your only goal is the lowest possible price and you can spare the day, the bus wins on the ticket.</p>

  <h2>Flying: fastest in the air, slow on the ground</h2>
  <p>SFO/OAK/SJC to LAX/BUR is only about 90 minutes in the air, and fares start around $60 — but the real number is higher once you add a checked bag ($35+), getting to the airport an hour or two early, and a ride from LAX on the other side. Door-to-door you're usually looking at 4–5 hours and $100+ all-in. Great when you find a genuinely cheap fare; less magical than it sounds otherwise.</p>

  <h2>Driving solo: flexible, but you pay for the whole tank</h2>
  <p>It's ~380 miles down I-5, about 6 hours. Fuel alone runs $50–$70 depending on your car and gas prices, before wear, tolls, and LA parking. You control the schedule and can stop wherever — but you're covering 100% of the cost and doing all the driving.</p>

  <h2>The train: beautiful, not practical</h2>
  <p>There's no fast direct Amtrak between SF and LA. The Coast Starlight is a scenic 11+ hour ride, and most "SF–LA" Amtrak trips involve a bus connection. Take it for the coastline, not to get somewhere on time.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <h3>🚗 The sweet spot: carpool</h3>
    <p>A carpool lands in the gap everything else misses — nearly bus-level price at roughly car-level speed. On Seat Sherpa you ride with someone <em>already making the drive</em> and split their gas at $40–$55 per seat, get there in about 6 hours, and get dropped closer to where you're actually going instead of an airport or bus terminal. No surge, no markup — you're sharing the real cost of a trip that's happening anyway.</p>
    <a class="btn" href="/routes/san-francisco-to-los-angeles">See SF → LA rides</a>
  </div>

  <h2>So which is cheapest?</h2>
  <p>Strictly by ticket price, the <strong>bus</strong> is cheapest — if you can give up the day. If you want cheap <em>and</em> fast, a <strong>carpool</strong> is the best-value option for most people: close to the bus on price, close to driving on time, and it drops you at a real address instead of a terminal. Flying only wins when you snag a rock-bottom fare and truly need the speed.</p>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <div class="faq"><h3>What is the cheapest way to get from San Francisco to Los Angeles?</h3><p>The bus has the lowest ticket ($20–$50) but takes 8–9 hours. A carpool ($40–$55/seat) is close on price and far faster (~6 hours), and drops you closer to your destination.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>Is it cheaper to fly or drive from SF to LA?</h3><p>Driving solo (~$50–$70 gas) is often cheaper than a flight once you add bags and airport transport — and much cheaper split across a carpool. Flying wins on cost only with a cheap fare.</p></div>
  <div class="faq"><h3>How long does it take to get from San Francisco to Los Angeles?</h3><p>About 6 hours by road (~380 miles via I-5). Flying is ~1.5 hours in the air but 4–5 door-to-door; the bus is 8–9 hours; Amtrak is 11+.</p></div>

